Lemon Financiers with Blackberry Jam
  --stirfrei, Food52

Jam:
1 pint blackberries
zest and juice of one lemon
3 thyme spirgs
1/4 c honey

Place all ingredients in a small saucepan and cook over low heat for 20-25 min or until the blackberries have softened and the juice has thickened. Remove thyme sprigs and place the jam in a food processor or blend with an immersion blender until smooth. Place in the refrigerator and cool until ready to use.

Financiers:
1/3 c rice flour
1/3 c almond flour
3/4 c confectioners sugar
3 egg whites
4 oz (1 stick) butter, cut into cubes
1/2 tsp vanilla
zest of one lemon

Preheat oven to 340F. Sift together rice flour, almond flour, and confectioner's sugar. Place into a large bowl. Make a well in the center. Froth egg whites with a whisk and place in the center of the well. Heat a small pan over high heat. Add butter and turn into beurre noisette. Allow the butter to melt and come to a boil. Constantly move the pan around until the milk solids have turned a golden brown color. Remove from heat and cool slightly before adding to the center of the well. Add vanilla and lemon zest. Whisk dough together to form a smooth paste. Butter a rectangular or boat shaped tartlet mold (I used a round one, so whatever you have around the house is fine.) Place on a baking sheet. Fill tart molds about 3/4 of the way full, using a portion scoop or a tbsp. Place in oven and bake for 15-20 min or until the paste has set. Remove from mold and let cool on a wire rack. Once financiers have cooled, place jam in a piping bag using the smallest tip. Place tip into bottom of financier and pipe a small amount of jam into each cake, ~1/2 tsp. Garnish cakes with confectioner's sugar. Cakes are best eaten the day of.



Notes: Used a muffin tin, made 9-10, started browning the butter first while sifting ingredients, whisked egg whites while pouring it in so that it didn't cook them


